    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `build_server()` in `app/server.py`, and both tools work when called over stdio from a real MCP client.\n\nThe pinned `mcp==2.2.0` is a major version newer than most examples you'll find. In 2.x, `FastMCP` was renamed to `MCPServer`, so the server is built with `MCPServer(\"notes\")` (from `mcp.server.mcpserver`) and the two tools are registered with `@server.tool()`.\n\n- **`add_note(title, body)`** stores the note in `NOTES` and returns `\"saved <title>\"`.\n- **`word_count(title)`** returns `len(NOTES[title].split())`.\n- **Unknown titles:** `word_count` raises `ToolError(\"no such note: ...\")`. In this SDK version, any other exception, such as a `KeyError`, is hidden from the model: it only sees \"Error executing tool word_count\". A `ToolError` passes its message through with `is_error=True`, so the model can read why the call failed.\n- **stdio:** `python -m app.server` still calls `build_server().run()`, which defaults to stdio.\n\n**Checks run:**\n- `pytest -q`: 1 passed. The existing test only checks that `NOTES` starts empty, so nothing in the test suite covers the new tools.\n- `python -m app.smoke` lists `add_note` and `word_count`.\n- A small client script over stdio got:\n  - `add_note(\"a\", \"one two  three\\nfour\")` \u2192 `saved a`\n  - `word_count(\"a\")` \u2192 `4`\n  - `word_count(\"zz\")` \u2192 error, `is_error=True`, message `Error executing tool word_count: no such note: 'zz'`\n\n**Cleanup needed:** the permission settings blocked deleting that client script, so `_e2e_check.py` is still in the project root. Please delete it, or turn it into a real test under `tests/` if you'd like coverage for the tools.\n\nNo dependencies were added or changed."
  },
